    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors like Lisinopril? I'm specifically trying to understand how they interact with the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) to reduce blood pressure.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors, such as Lisinopril, work by blocking the action of Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE), an enzyme that converts Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor. By blocking this conversion, ACE inhibitors prevent the formation of Angiotensin II, which is a key component of the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) that regulates blood pressure. As a result, the vasoconstrictive effects of Angiotensin II are reduced, leading to vasodilation and decreased blood pressure. This action also reduces aldosterone levels, further contributing to the blood pressure-lowering effect.",
      "source": "target",
