    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the specific steps of the RAAS cascade? I'm a little confused about how the reduction in Angiotensin II actually leads to the decrease in aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The RAAS cascade typically proceeds from Renin to Angiotensin II to Angiotensin II Type I Receptor (AT1R). Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitors primarily inhibit this Angiotensin II pathway. Angiotensin II's downstream effect is through its binding to AT1R. When AT1R is activated by Angiotensin II, it triggers a signaling cascade that ultimately increases secretory statistics, promoting aldosterone release. ACE inhibitors block the formation of Angiotensin II, which reduces the signaling cascade initiated by AT1R. Lower Angiotensin II levels lead to decreased aldosterone secretion and activation of Anti-Vasopressin/neural peptide pathways leading to more aquaporin playing more freely.",
      "source": "target",
